-- substitution-on-checked-syntax (2026-11-14): docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, §6.1
-- “Substitution on checked syntax”: a well-typed substitution (a checked term per variable, a canonical
-- form per dimension) takes a checked term or type in Γ to one in Δ whose meaning is the substitution's
-- action on the original's, [[t[σ]]]ρ ≡ [[t]][[σ]]ρ by refl; derivations also rename along worlds
-- (ren-der). Resolved 2026-11-14 (lib/capacities/nbe.cub: DSubst, subst-tm, subst-ty, subst-sound, ren-der;
-- example: x := true in not x gives false).
substitution-on-checked-syntax : Node
substitution-on-checked-syntax = node "Checked syntax substitutes: a well-typed substitution takes a checked term to a checked term whose meaning is the substitution's action on the original's." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- path-free-transport (2026-11-14): transport along a line of types at every path-free shape (Bool,
-- Nat, Σ, Π): Der's dtransp (code ccomp (cplam cL) csys0 c) with its meaning the host's comp, and fund's
-- case KT by induction on the shape: at Bool and Nat the value (computability ignores the type
-- component, One1), at Σ componentwise, at Π through the clause's AppV data (zpt, via-snd), the
-- argument transported back along the domain and the result along the codomain at its fill. Resolved
-- 2026-11-14 (lib/capacities/nbe.cub, examples: transp at Bool, Nat, Σ Bool Bool and Bool → Bool normalize).
path-free-transport : Node
path-free-transport = node "Transport along a line of types at every path-free shape is a typed code of the normalizer over cubes: computable, and it normalizes." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- Known (2026-11-14, checked): the normalizer as it stands (Nat, Unit, Empty, List's constructors,
-- transport at every path-free shape, substitution on checked syntax) checks from nothing in an empty
-- store: experiments/nbe/examples.cub, 1767 passed, 0 failed, 2815M interaction steps, 18.7 minutes.
-- composition-with-systems (2026-11-14): the object language's comp A [φ ↦ u] a0 with φ over the
-- context's dimensions, transport at PathP (composition in the fibre with the endpoints' system), then
-- Glue and universes. Its meaning is host comp with the face over the context cube's dimensions; a
-- checked definition writes it on a fresh local dimension (λ k → comp A [k = 1 ↦ u'] a0) and
-- path-applies that line at the face's form, the host substituting into faces (tested 2026-11-14).
-- That covers a system of a fixed number of faces. The formal compositions this produces have
-- systems whose number of faces grows with the shape (each PathP layer adds two; eliminators commute
-- with formal composition), and a checked definition writes a system only as fixed syntax (sys+ is a
-- syntactic chain in src/lang.c, not a value of a type), so at every shape this needs systems (or faces
-- and partial elements) as values in the host, or a way to build such systems without them. This was
-- wrongly put to Avik as a choice (2026-11-14); it is the session's to settle. CCHM's composition
-- takes a system, which CCHM's rules hold as judgmental syntax; an internal type of partial elements
-- (Cubical Agda's Partial φ A) is not in CCHM's rules, so adding one to the core has to pass the
-- minimality test (is the construction impossible without it?). Not yet settled: whether these growing
-- systems can be built without host partial elements, from fixed systems or as data in the
-- normalizer's own cube model, before any change to the core is considered.
-- Analysis (2026-11-14, from lib/core/check.cub and the machine): the machine already holds systems as
-- values (a partial value is a list of face and branch, wsys bs, read by %view); only the checker
-- lacks a type for them: check-sys accepts a system only as syntax (tsys) inside a composition's
-- tube, Glue or a composition of types, checking each branch under each clause of its face and every
-- overlap judgmentally at generic dimensions. Consequences, each followed through: (1) one face of
-- any DNF form works with fixed syntax (a fresh dimension k, [k = 1 ↦ u'], path-applied at the face's
-- form); (2) two or more branches on fresh dimensions fail, because their overlap k1 = 1 ∧ k2 = 1 is
-- checked at generic k and the branches are unrelated there; (3) faces k = 0 and k = 1 of one fresh
-- dimension are disjoint and need no overlap check, but only express disjoint faces; (4) folding n
-- branches into two by the filler of the first n − 1 (comp [φn ↦ un, ψ ↦ fill of the rest]) is a valid
-- Kan composition but again needs two overlapping branches of dynamic faces, so (2) applies. The
-- object language's systems are lists of branches of any length (lib/core/code.cub's ccomp), so the meaning
-- of an object composition, not only the Kan lemma, needs a system of length computed at run time.
-- So the construction is impossible without a checked type for the systems the machine already has:
-- the minimality test is met, and the change exposes in the checker what the core's values already
-- are rather than adding a value. The standard presentation is Cubical Agda's (IsOne φ, Partial φ A,
-- PartialP, a system literal as a term of Partial φ A with overlaps checked, the tube of comp a line
-- of them). Not yet settled: faces as values need an interval-valued term built by recursion, and the
-- interval is not a type of this host (I × Nat is refused); faces would come only from bound
-- dimensions and path application at forms, as the normalizer's cubes already build them.
-- Checked further (2026-11-15): a type of partial elements alone does not make this writable. A face
-- computed from the object term reaches the host either as an interval value computed from data,
-- which the host cannot produce (no function returns an interval), or as a fresh bound dimension
-- later path-applied at the face's form (consequence (1)). With fresh stand-ins, a Partial over
-- k1 = 1 ∨ k2 = 1 is checked on the overlap k1 = 1 ∧ k2 = 1 at generic k, where the branches are
-- unrelated, which is consequence (2) again: the object branches agree only on φ1 ∧ φ2, after
-- substitution. So what the construction forces is two things together: interval-valued terms (the
-- interval as a sort functions return, Cubical Agda's I as a pretype; in CCHM the interval appears
-- only in contexts) and a type of partial elements over such faces (IsOne φ, Partial φ A). Both are
-- interpreted directly in the cubical-set model CCHM is the syntax of: the interval is the representable
-- interval, a face a map to the face lattice, Partial φ A the exponential by the face's subobject; so
-- they arise in the mathematics and add no semantics. Partial helps only where a system's meaning comes
-- as one partial element on the whole face (the derivation's premise judged on φ1 ∨ … ∨ φn); joining
-- branches inside the host meets the generic-dimension agreement check again.
-- Taken up (2026-11-15, Avik: a change a completion requirement forces is the session's, never put to
-- him): the core gains interval-valued terms and a type of partial elements. Tested state: checked code
-- cannot name the interval as a type at all (pick : Bool → I → I → I is refused, "not a bound variable
-- or checked definition: I"), and systems are checked only as syntax (check-sys on tsys) inside a
-- composition's tube, Glue and a composition of types.
-- Derivation continued (2026-11-15), correcting the "both forced" above. (a) Any function that builds a
-- system from data is checked once, at generic arguments, so its faces are neutral there (face-of b
-- for a variable b), whether they come as interval values returned by a function or as bound
-- dimensions in continuation-passing; interval-valued terms alone do not remove the generic overlap
-- check. (b) What the overlap check needs is reasoning under a neutral face: in a context assuming
-- φ = 1 for a neutral φ, a composition whose system has the branch [φ ↦ p] reduces to that branch's
-- end (CCHM's boundary rule read under an assumed cofibration; Cubical Agda checks overlaps this way,
-- with IsOne φ in the context). The current checker substitutes 0 or 1 for dimension variables only.
-- (c) With (b), branches need not agree judgmentally as given: the object branches' agreement reaches
-- the host as a path (Der's indices are forded), and each branch after the first can be corrected
-- along that path by a one-branch composition on the earlier face, after which it agrees with the
-- earlier branch under the overlap by the boundary rule. So the forced change is: faces over interval
-- terms that can be neutral (which needs interval-valued terms: the interval as a sort a function
-- returns), and the checker's conversion under an assumed face with neutral interval terms. Whether a
-- type of partial elements (Partial φ A) is also forced: settled (2026-11-15), it is. Nested
-- compositions cannot stand for one over a disjunction: under a neutral ψ = φ2 ∨ φ3 assumed 1 the
-- checker cannot tell which disjunct holds, so a composition over the rest never reduces by the
-- boundary rule. The data-dependent part is one partial element on the computed disjunction, joined
-- from branches (Cubical Agda's primPOr: overlap checked under φ1 ∧ φ2 assumed, which (b) and (c)
-- make hold), and a function that builds it from a list returns it, or passes it to a continuation,
-- as a value: that needs its type. So the forced change is Cubical Agda's: faces as interval terms
-- (φ : I, the interval a sort that terms of function type return), IsOne φ and Partial φ A with the
-- join of partial elements, comp taking a line of partial elements on one face, and conversion under
-- an assumed face whose interval terms may be neutral (the machine's interval values gain neutral atoms).
-- Known (2026-11-14, tested): the host's comp at Bool or Nat with a nonempty system on a variable is
-- a formal composition (hcompD), and a case or recursor on a formal composition reduces to the
-- formal composition of its branches along the motive at the composition's fill (eliminators
-- commute with it), so a formal composition is a normal form like a constructor, not a neutral: Bool's
-- and Nat's clauses get a formal-composition alternative (its base and tubes computable paths, its
-- face a canonical form), the eliminators' cases on it need composition along the motive, and the Kan
-- lemma is over composition with a boundary system on a path dimension (k = 0, k = 1, total tubes)
-- with its fills, by induction on the path-free shapes; transport at PathP is that composition.
-- Known (2026-11-14, tested): object-level data types beyond Nat by a description of their
-- constructors (a host data type Mu D whose field is ElD D (Mu D)) are refused by the host's
-- positivity check ("the field type is not strictly positive"), which does not unfold a type computed
-- by recursion on a parameter; each further data type is a shape of its own, as Nat is.
composition-with-systems : Node
composition-with-systems = node "Composition with a system on the context's faces, transport at PathP, Glue and universes in the normalizer over cubes." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- checker-returns-derivations (2026-11-14): a missed requirement of typed-codes-and-normalization
-- (its statement names "a checker that returns typing derivations"; no child covered it): a checker
-- over raw codes that returns a derivation (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, §6.1
-- “Typed stages and checking”: check(Γ, c, A) : Dec(Der(Γ, c, A)), or a checked term or a diagnostic).
-- Known (2026-11-14): a derivation is indexed by its meaning, so the checker must produce meanings:
-- a λ's from its body (Der's dlam takes the body over lam-ctx, which is built from the λ's own
-- meaning; a rule over the type's extension, with its meaning the body abstracted by dcube-pull and
-- fund's case by a β lemma at the cube level, breaks the circle), an elimination's from its
-- arguments'; and conversion by comparing normal forms needs a normal code to determine its meaning,
-- which it does only up to the type component of Bool's and Nat's values (One1, which has no
-- judgmental uniqueness in the host).
-- Design (2026-11-14, taken up): every meaning the checker produces is pointwise, dcube-of g over the
-- context's cube for a function g of the vertex and environment (varCube already is). A rule's
-- conclusion meaning, built from its premises' by fmapM, extM, lam-body and the like, is a path away
-- from the pointwise one by the "of" lemmas (dcube-map-of, fmap-of, push-of, pull-of, carry-of), and
-- der-tr moves a derivation along such a path. The λ circle breaks because a pointwise conclusion's
-- extended context depends only on its type part: dcube-map-of takes lam-ctx Γ a b (dcube-of g) to
-- the context extended pointwise by the expected domain, where the body is checked before the λ's
-- meaning exists; the body's derivation is carried to lam-ctx along that path of contexts with its
-- meaning by carry-of. Types compare by their codes: equal codes give one result of the checker, so
-- a path of codes is a path of the pointwise meanings (cong). One1 components are tt1 pointwise.
-- Refined (2026-11-14, in experiments/nbe/check-wip.cub): checking mode takes the expected type as a
-- meaning M of some Y with h : Y → Sem s reading the type off it (a type code's meaning: Sem s and the
-- identity; an argument's: the function's meaning and its domain), and returns the element cube E
-- over M (ElOver: a cube over the base paired with M) with a derivation at pairM M E (one
-- dcube-pull). Done and checked: application over a checked argument (appR, one pull over the
-- function's meaning; appR-f by fmap-pull, pull-ignore and fmap-id, appR-a by fmap-pull; dapp-c).
-- Done and checked: the switch from synthesis to checking (conv-E, conv-path): a synthesized meaning
-- S and a path of type cubes to (Y, h, M) give the element cube over M and a path from S to its
-- meaning as checked, by S's own elements lifted (selfE, pull-lift0), the move to the cube of types
-- and back (pull-fm over dmap-fmap) and the host's transport along the type path, each step a
-- carry with its fill. ElOver is over the cube of the types h reads off M (phih). Next: variables
-- (dvar at varCube) with their type paths, for the fragment of closed simple types first (a type's
-- meaning constant over the context, so the paths are of-lift and a lift over a section's cube),
-- then the checker as a function over codes.
-- Plan for the simple-types fragment (2026-11-14; types t ::= bool | t → t with constant meanings
-- sem t): a context is a list of simple types ts, its cube cube-map (into ts) (cube0 ts) over a cube
-- of One1 (into ts builds the context value with the constant types), so the environments over a
-- vertex have constant types judgmentally. Every meaning over it is a reindexing
-- dcube-reidx (into ts) of a pointwise dcube-of g over cube0 ts with g's type part sem t
-- judgmentally; then application, variables and the types need no conversion at all: each rule's
-- premise meanings are path-equal to such cubes by lemmas proved by plain induction on the
-- dimension (fmap over a reindexing, map over a reindexing, dcube-of over a mapped cube as a
-- reindexing, dcube-map-of), and lam-ctx of a pointwise λ is cube-map (into (t ∷ ts)) of the same
-- cube once mapped (the λ's type part is constant), so the body is checked in ctx-of (t ∷ ts) and
-- carried to lam-ctx with push over a reindexing. The general machinery above (conv-E, conv-path)
-- is for dependent types after this fragment.
-- Done and checked (2026-11-14, experiments/nbe/check-wip.cub): the checker ck over codes for the
-- simple-types fragment (variables by lookw and vder, application by app-ok, λ by lam-ok checked at
-- a function type, the switch from synthesis to checking by comparing simple types), returning a
-- derivation at the constant meaning or none; the context is an argument after the code, since a
-- changing implicit index before it ran the checker out of memory. Every case is checked by the
-- host, so a derivation it returns is one. Example end to end: the raw code λ x. x checked at
-- Bool → Bool, then normalized to λ x. x; λ f. f at (Bool → Bool) → Bool → Bool normalizes to its
-- η-long form λ f. λ x. f x. Found and fixed (2026-11-14/15): normalizing what the checker returned for
-- a term with two binders or an application did not finish (λ f. λ x. x ran out of memory; λ f. λ x.
-- f x crashed the machine, then, with the machine reporting a case that meets a foreign constructor,
-- read a variable in an empty context). The defect was the machine's transport on a constructor of
-- an indexed family, which moved a recursive field (a premise derivation, at its own index) along the
-- outer index's line (fixed in src/lang.c; the small witness is at the composition finding of the
-- trusted-part review). Now λ f. λ x. f x and λ f. λ x. x check and normalize to themselves.
-- Done and checked (2026-11-15): case on Bool (case-ok: the scrutinee at Bool, both branches at t,
-- the motive the constant type t over the context extended by a Bool, dty-s carried to ext-h as λ's
-- body is; bcase-at and case-m for checking and synthesis). Examples: case true of false, true
-- normalizes to false; λ b. case b of false, true is its own normal form (1724 passed, 0 failed).
-- Done and checked (2026-11-15): products t1 × t2 in the fragment (dty-sig for the type; pair-ok with
-- the second component's type the constant t2 over the context extended by t1; fst-ok and snd-ok;
-- pairs checked or synthesized, projections synthesized). Examples: the swap λ p. (snd p , fst p) at
-- Bool × Bool → Bool × Bool is its own normal form; fst (true , false) normalizes to true; a pair at
-- Bool is refused (1736 passed, 0 failed).
-- Resolved (2026-11-15): ck calls itself on a case's branches through the nested eliminator below;
-- negation, case true of false, true and λ a b. case a of b, false check through ck and normalize
-- (check-wip.cub 1749 passed, 0 failed). Was (2026-11-15, tested): ck cannot call itself on a case's branches. Code is a nested inductive
-- type (ccase, ccon, cdata, celim hold their subterms in a Lst Code), and checked structural
-- recursion takes hypotheses only for fields of type D itself, under paths, Π codomains and Σ
-- components: csz (ccase d (lcons t r) x) = suc (csz t) is refused, csx (ccase d B x) = suc (csx x)
-- checks. So every raw-syntax construct with a list of subterms is out of reach of a checked
-- function over codes. What is missing is the eliminator of a nested inductive type: a field of type
-- L D, L a data type whose one parameter occurs strictly positively, takes as hypothesis the L-shaped
-- tuple of the hypotheses of its D elements (per L, a type %ihall-L P x by case on x and its maker
-- %ihmap-L h x; a shape sh-nest in lib/core/check.cub's rec-shape, hyp-val, hyp-ty, about 25 lines; a
-- shape 'N' in src/lang.c's rec_shape and src/net.c's ih_maker, about 30 lines; the two generated
-- definitions in src/lang.c, about 40 lines; in src/syntax.c a split of such a field reverting its
-- hypothesis into the split's motive so that it unfolds to the pair of its head's hypothesis and its
-- tail's, and a call on the head read as the pair's first component, about 80 lines). Then ck's case
-- clause is one line. Next: that eliminator, then dependent types through conv-E, then the checker
-- into lib/capacities/nbe.cub.
-- Done (2026-11-15), as designed here: a field of type L D, L a data type with one parameter
-- and no indices whose constructors' fields are the parameter itself (an element), L at it (a recursive
-- field) or not mentioning it, D at its own parameters, has shape N (the machine's rsh 'N' and L's index;
-- lib/core/check.cub's sh-nest). Its hypothesis is %ihn-L h x, generated with L: by cases on x, the
-- tuple of h applied to each element field and %ihn-L h at each recursive field, right-nested pairs
-- ending in Unit-at's constructor (Unit-at l : Type l, lib/core/primitives.cub, possible since data
-- types may declare a universe over a parameter). Its type is %iht-L l P x, generated with it, the
-- same cases into Type l. Both are eliminations of x, stuck on a variable and unfolding when it is
-- split; the machine and the checker use the same generated functions, so the stuck forms are
-- identical. A split of a field with such a hypothesis reverts the hypothesis into the split's motive
-- (src/syntax.c), and a call on an element bound in the split reads the hypothesis's component.
-- Implemented: src/lang.c nest_container, nest_of, gen_nest (emits the checked %iht-L and %ihn-L once
-- Unit-at and L are checked; Lst, List and Opt get them in lib/core/primitives.cub), the shape N<L>;
-- in ConInfo.rsh; src/net.c's case rule applies %ihn-L to the elimination as a function; the
-- operations %diht and %dihn give the checker the generated definitions; lib/core/check.cub's
-- sh-nest (rec-shape-top, at a field's top only, as the machine), is-rec-ty counting nested fields
-- (programs over codes see them), branch-hyps typing the hypothesis; src/syntax.c's Nest entries
-- and direct Deep entries, the hypothesis reverted at a split of the field; course-of-values
-- recursion leaves nested fields out of its values below. experiments/nested/rose.cub: count on a
-- rose tree by clauses through lcons patterns. Library 1953 passed with the 24 known failures.
-- A recursion over a container of any length takes the field's hypothesis (rec ks in a clause) and a
-- function over the tuple, its type named %iht-L (experiments/nested/rose.cub: size). Found with it
-- (2026-11-15): conversion compared levels structurally when no type was at hand (a data type's level
-- argument, a stuck elimination's motive): Unit-at (lmax lzero lzero) and Unit-at lzero differed. Now
-- two level values compare as levels there too (lib/core/check.cub uconv-head, is-level-d).
checker-returns-derivations : Node
checker-returns-derivations = node "A checker takes raw codes to typing derivations (or a diagnostic), as the paper's §6.1 specifies." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- typed-codes-after-composition (2026-11-14): everything typed codes and normalization by evaluation
-- need after composition with systems: Glue, universes (a level-stratified TyE, since El lands in Type
-- and a universe's elements are types), and further object-level data types, each a shape of its own.
-- Known (2026-11-14): List's recursor (and any eliminator of a data type with a parameter) pairs the
-- motive's data with the scrutinee's clause data, whose element types agree only along paths; zpt
-- carries the scrutinee to the motive's element type (as Π's clause does), and the case of fund
-- needs zpt's congruence in its argument, its naturality in fibrewise maps that commute with
-- transport, and zpt along the path that subM-id and fmapM-fmapM give being the identity pairing.
typed-codes-after-composition : Node
typed-codes-after-composition = node "Everything typed codes and normalization by evaluation need after composition with systems: Glue, universes and further object-level data types." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- unit-empty-list (2026-11-14): Unit, Empty (with absurd) and List (nil, cons; its recursor not yet:
-- see typed-codes-after-composition) as object-level types of the normalizer over cubes. Resolved
-- 2026-11-14 for Unit and Empty (lib/capacities/nbe.cub; examples: tt, a Unit variable, absurd on an Empty
-- variable) and List's constructors (examples: a list literal, a list variable).
unit-empty-list : Node
unit-empty-list = node "Unit, Empty with absurd, and List's constructors are object-level types of the normalizer over cubes: computable, and they normalize." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- object-level-nat (2026-11-14): Nat as the first object-level data type: shape s-nat, zero, suc
-- and the recursor (step a term at Π k:Nat. P k → P (suc k), motive a type over the extension by
-- Nat) in Der and NN, the clause CNat, fund's recursor case nrec-comp by induction on the scrutinee's
-- clause. Resolved 2026-11-14: lib/capacities/nbe.cub with experiments/nbe/examples.cub (nrec on 2 normalizes to
-- suc (suc zero), nrec on a variable stays neutral) checks fresh: 1540 passed, 0 failed, 2294M steps.
object-level-nat : Node
object-level-nat = node "Nat is an object-level data type of the normalizer over cubes: its constructors and recursor are typed codes, computable, and normalize." (cons typed-codes-after-swap nil)
-- language-implementation-remainder (2026-11-14): everything the paper specifies for the language
-- that typed codes and normalization by evaluation do not cover. What is known about it follows
-- (moved here from the language goal's statement when the goal was expanded).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The implementation is shown to be
-- exactly the published calculus, rule by rule, and every deviation is fixed or justified. nobody
-- has compared the implementation with the published calculus, so whether it is "implemented fully
-- correctly" is unknown. Read the evaluator's rule cases (src/net.c), the translation of terms into
-- its graph (src/lang.c), the composition code generated per data type as unchecked text
-- (src/lang.c), universe composition (lib/core/prelude.cub, %hcompU, Cubical Agda's design, not CCHM's
-- Glue) and the checker's typing clauses (lib/core/check.cub) against CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber,
-- Mörtberg) and the clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi), recording each rule as
-- matching or deviating, then fix or justify each deviation. What is known follows. The deliverable
-- is not an agent's statement that the code is right: it is the trusted part made small enough to
-- be plainly the published rules, each rule case shown beside the rule it implements, everything
-- else checked or unable to change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work. For every data declaration this generates, as
-- untyped source text, the type's composition (%comp-D), its homogeneous composition, and transport
-- on each constructor and on formal compositions. That text is never checked: it is trusted as
-- CCHM's rules for the declared type, written once in C per declaration rather than as the single
-- schematic rule CCHM gives for inductive and higher inductive types. Show it is exactly that rule,
-- or replace the per-declaration generation by the schematic rule.
-- Evidence (2026-11-14, from the checker that returns derivations): normalizing a derivation that was
-- transported along a path of its indices (Der, an indexed family, transported with its fields'
-- derivations nested) reads a variable in an empty context (a case on Empty met none), and with two
-- nested λ runs out of memory. The machine's composition of an indexed family is the per-declaration
-- generated text this finding names; a wrong field or index there gives exactly such a value of one
-- type read as another. Checking that text against the schematic rule, or replacing it, is now
-- needed by typed codes and normalization as well.
-- Found and fixed (2026-11-15, src/lang.c, transport on a constructor): a recursive field was
-- transported along the outer type's parameter lines rather than along its own type's arguments.
-- For a parameterized type (a field List A of List A) the two agree; for an indexed family they do
-- not. Smallest witness: data V : Type → Type with v1 : (A : Type) → A → V A and vu : Nat → V Nat →
-- V Bool; transporting vu 0 (v1 Nat 1) along V (ua not) moved the inner v1's index equation (a path
-- Nat ≡ Nat) to end at a Glue over Bool, a value of V Nat made into one of V Bool. Now each argument of
-- the field's type is a line, with the parameters and the earlier fields at i ∧ j, which is CCHM's
-- transport on a constructor read at the field's own type. This was one defect of the generated text;
-- the rest of it is still unchecked against the schematic rule.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work. The calculus's rules are the cases of this function
-- (application, projections, path application, composition, Glue, interval and faces, systems,
-- higher-constructor boundaries, hcomp, transport, case: about 650 lines), the face algebra
-- (src/dnf.c), the composition clauses for the type formers (lib/core/prelude.cub) and the composition
-- code generated per data type (generated in src/lang.c). But the rule bodies also make
-- sharing, deferral and memoization decisions inline (V_DEFER, boxes and references, A8 free-
-- dimension cuts), and what a value is depends on the lazy-copy and substitution machinery above
-- being right. So the part that must be trusted is in effect this whole file. To make the
-- implementation's conformance to the published rules checkable by reading: the rules alone
-- determine results, and the machinery is confined to scheduling, sharing and identity, where by
-- construction (strong confluence of interaction nets; memo keys that are the full content of a
-- deterministic computation) it cannot change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/core/prelude.cub): needs-work. CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, “Cubical Type Theory: a constructive interpretation of the univalence axiom”, arXiv:1611.02108) §7.1 “Composition for the universe” defines composition in the universe
-- as a Glue type along the equivalences given by transport (comp^i U [φ ↦ E] A0 = Glue [φ ↦ (E(i1),
-- equiv_i E(1−i))] A0). Here it is instead its own type former, %hcompU, with unglue on a face as
-- transport back along that face's line (%unglue-line) and its own composition (%comp-gluel,
-- %tproof): the design of Cubical Agda (Vezzosi, Mörtberg, Abel). The extra face ¬φ ↦ A0 is
-- admissible (on ¬φ the tube is empty and the composite is A0; φ ∧ ¬φ is 0 in the face lattice),
-- and makes a closed composition of types compute to a type. Not yet read against Cubical Agda's
-- published rules for this type former, its unglue and its composition; until then these four
-- clauses are trusted without a reference reading.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading the
-- evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side
-- with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the
-- clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as
-- matching or deviating at its site. The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until
-- this is done, conformance of the implementation to the calculus is not known.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's
-- translation of terms into the evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning
-- (the strictness analysis that decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting,
-- sweeping of compiled code, the module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and
-- whose hits are trusted).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading
-- lib/core/check.cub's typing clauses against the calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or
-- deviating at its site.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The specializer carries its law in
-- its type, and the three Futamura projections hold (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6 “Reflective programming and language extension”: eq. (6.4) “the specialization equation”;
-- §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”: eqs. (6.6) “the first Futamura projection” and (6.7) “the second Futamura projection” and "any further projection"; §6.2 “Specialization as a program over the language’s own definitions”). The specializer
-- normalizes through a primitive that runs the untyped checker, so it cannot be specialized itself;
-- with normalization as a term (typed codes, the sibling node) it carries its law in its type and the three Futamura
-- projections follow. Specializing a process through a handler was measured superlinear
-- (lib/capacities/specialize.cub, 2026-11-09) and is unattributed. It waits on the typed codes above.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/capacities/specialize.cub): needs-work. Specialization cost: #build of a process
-- written through a handler was measured superlinear in the number of commands (2026-11-09: 7
-- commands 42M steps, 22 commands 1.21 billion steps, 3 min 39 s; experiments/os/build-cost*.cub
-- hold the per-stage probes). Not attributed (normalization, rewriting, or the passes of
-- lib/capacities/x86.cub). It is a cost of the language's own programs about programs.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/capacities/specialize.cub): needs-work. Normalization here is code-norm-at, a
-- primitive that runs the evaluator's own quoter (lib/library/std.cub's checker services). The
-- specializer is therefore not a term all the way down, and cannot be specialized itself: the
-- second and third Futamura projections (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”: the second
-- is eq. (6.7) “the second Futamura projection”; "the same stage and representation indices apply to any further projection") need
-- normalization as a term of the language (normalization by evaluation over typed codes, with its
-- soundness equation, ibid. eq. (6.5) “reifySound”).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Native code replaces a definition only with the relation to its meaning
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9 “Native execution as a mathematical program transformation”). Native code today comes from an x86 translator whose output is bound and
-- run with no relation to the definition (src/lang.c, lib/capacities/x86.cub), from pasted machine code
-- (lib/capacities/elf.cub), and with proofs about its bytes at one fixed placement (lib/capacities/compile/); nothing
-- relates a fleet's proved behaviour to its binaries (lib/examples/deploy.cub). Decided by Avik
-- (2026-11-14): native realization goes through C. "are you going to complete writing proven-
-- correct x86 RISC ARM impls instead of writing a C impl ? the value delivered to me by a complete
-- x86 machine code impl is near-zero. cant even run on a macbook can it ... there remains no
-- argument for x86 as far as i can tell." One C emitter runs wherever a C compiler does; per-ISA
-- emitters and their proofs would multiply by every target. The paper permits it (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export": "C and C++ text are optional target presentations. If an external compiler produces the executed bytes,
-- either its relevant compilation relation is assumed or those bytes receive their own checked
-- connection"). What follows: the emitter returns the relation between running the emitted C and
-- the term's meaning, which needs the meaning of the C fragment the emitter produces (a small,
-- fixed subset), not of all of C (that stays language support); the C compiler's relation is a
-- named assumption listed by #trusted; the evaluator substitutes native code only under that
-- relation. The x86 translator (src/lang.c, lib/capacities/x86.cub, lib/capacities/machine.cub, lib/capacities/elf.cub's pasted
-- machine code, lib/capacities/compile/) is superseded by it. The record that preceded the decision: plan
-- revision 9 (archive/2026-11-11/PLAN-rev9.md, revision 9, 9 Nov 2026, sha256 4d012c3a10fd…, §5 “The distinctions everything above x86 rests on”, item 5 “The target is x86-64”) had chosen x86 ("Not LLVM IR,
-- not C"); a session on 2026-11-12 planned C to break a cycle in its goal graph; neither was
-- Avik's.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): wrong, against the paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export"): the
-- evaluator may replace a definition by native code only once a target-correctness witness relating
-- the code's execution to the definition exists. Here the bytes are bound after the only check
-- made, that the specialized term converts with the definition (lib/capacities/x86.cub jit-plan); the
-- translation from that term to bytes (x-push, x-lift, x-erase, the templates, this boundary's
-- marshaling) carries no relation, so a wrong translation would answer wrongly with nothing to
-- catch it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/examples/deploy.cub): needs-work. fl-beh (what lib/examples/fleet.cub's properties are
-- stated of) and fl-node (the process each binary runs) are both built from cp-view, but nothing
-- relates them: the properties proved of the behaviour are not shown to hold of the binaries. The
-- relation comes from processes realized as executables with their system-call events equal to the
-- process's interaction (native realization, above), applied here; nothing specific to
-- this file is owed.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/capacities/elf.cub): needs-work. The driver loop, the #build-loop round driver and
-- the start code below are machine code assembled from tools/asm/*.s and pasted as byte literals:
-- trusted as written, with no statement of what they do. They go when processes become native executables by the route
-- native realization takes (above).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/capacities/compile/meaning.cub): needs-work. This file, lib/capacities/compile/runs.cub,
-- lib/capacities/compile/tmpl.cub, lib/capacities/compile/process.cub's kernel (kn-step, a second kernel beside
-- lib/languages/os/contract.cub's) and experiments/isa/addw-all.cub (one all-inputs theorem, for the bytes of
-- word addition) are proofs about the output of the x86 translator at one fixed placement, a form
-- the paper replaces (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”: Block, eq. (9.12);
-- seq, eq. (9.13); the compiler as a fold, eq. (9.15), with the relational congruence, eq. (9.16)). They are to be deleted with that
-- translator; the word, bit and flag law files beside them and x86ok's run lemmas remain
-- usable for x86 as a later target.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every law of the primitives is
-- proved, or named as an assumption (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, "Exact scalar
-- types"). lib/bootstrap/prim.cub postulates word and bit laws that
-- are provable from the unbounded definitions; fixed-width types have no specification; floating
-- point is trusted without a named assumption (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/bootstrap/prim.cub): needs-work. Of the 91 primitives here, the laws of word
-- multiplication, division, remainder and the bitwise operations, the wbit-* bit laws, the byte
-- load/store laws, array extensionality and the bit round trips are postulated, though words are
-- unbounded naturals and these laws are provable from the inductive definitions (lib/library/std.cub
-- already proves addition, equality, order, maximum and subtraction equal to theirs). The fixed-
-- width arithmetic (Int64, UInt32, UInt64) has no specification at all, and floating point is
-- wholly trusted. The paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, "Exact scalar types"):
-- a native primitive implements its definition under a checked theorem or a named assumption. Prove what is provable; define U_w = Fin(2^w) with its modular operations
-- and relate each fixed-width primitive to it or name the assumption; name IEEE binary64 as an
-- assumption. ---------------------------------------------------------------- the types
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Deriving is a typed term over the
-- code of a data declaration. lib/library/derive.cub's 24 definitions read the checker's own values and are
-- refused as untyped, so `deriving` is off and the Rust readers fail behind it. Deriving becomes a
-- term over the code of a data declaration, which needs a declaration's code as a value (work-as-a-
-- value). It waits on the work being a value (product implementation).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/library/derive.cub): needs-work. 24 definitions here are untyped and refused by
-- the checker, which has rejected untyped definitions outside the bootstrap files since 2026-11-09,
-- deliberately, after about 10,000 lines of untyped code were written by an agent. They are to be
-- written with types; nothing in the checker changes. `deriving eq` in the surface language depends
-- on this file and is off until it checks. 2026-11-12: they cannot be given types as they are: they
-- read the checker's own values (a constructor's telescope tx A nm r, tval, %var, %view (%gval g),
-- from lib/core/check.cub, a bootstrap file whose value types are not checked data), which no type of a
-- checked definition names. Typed, deriving is a term over a code of the data type's declaration
-- (its constructors and field types, as Code), not over the checker's values.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The evaluator handles terms of any
-- depth without quadratic cost or stack overflow. measured 2026-11-09, building a term cost
-- quadratic time in its depth and deep terms overflowed the C stack (src/net.c). Measured
-- 2026-11-13 at depths 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000: 2.6 s, 1.5 s, 4.0 s, so no longer quadratic there;
-- the stack at depth 10^6 is not re-measured.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): question. Measured 2026-11-09: build_m calls synkey at every
-- node it builds and synkey walks the whole subterm, so building a term of depth d costs d^2 key
-- computations (a unary numeral of 4*10^4 did not print in 260 s), and deep right-nested terms (a
-- numeral of 10^6) overflow the C stack in several recursive functions of this file and src/lang.c.
-- Not re-measured since; whether both still hold is not known.
-- Resolved (2026-11-14, src/syntax.c): a file with one unbalanced parenthesis (the graph's
-- essential-context relation, one closing parenthesis short) seemed to exit with status 1 and print
-- nothing. The reason was printed, on stderr only and before the buffered lines of the definitions
-- checked so far, so a reader of stdout saw nothing and a merged stream had it mid-line. Text that
-- cannot be read now ends the load through the one error path: the checked lines first, then the
-- reason last, on stderr and on stdout when that is another file; a missing closing parenthesis or
-- brace names where it was opened (expected ) (closing the one opened at 4:11), found :).
-- Resolved (2026-11-14, lib/core/check.cub): the library's typed transport applied to a line of types
-- that is a case on a path into Bool (false-true A e = transport (λ i → if e i then A else (A → A))
-- (λ a → a)) was refused with "an implicit argument is not determined by the other arguments'
-- types or the type expected": the λ, guessed a function, was skipped where a path was due, and
-- nothing else determined transport's implicit types. An application whose implicit arguments
-- nothing else determines now guesses such a λ again as a line, and the line's ends at 0 and 1
-- solve the path type's ends; false-true checks.
-- Known (2026-11-14, from Avik): the repository holds four kinds of thing, kept apart: the core
-- (the primitive language), capacities demonstrated and mentioned in the paper (each a metacircular
-- extension on the unchanged core, its correctness proved in the language), examples of what the
-- language can do, and our usage (code that exists because building hyperactivity uses it now, with
-- no claim of the paper behind it). Every core term is flagged as
-- core, and files are organized by layer, so the layer of a construction is visible where it stands.
-- Done (2026-11-15): data types at a universe that depends on a parameter. A declared universe
-- (data D ps : … → Type l) is the type's level, a function of the parameters (the entry dentl: a
-- level telescope over the parameters; lib/core/check.cub's dlvl-at), with the fields checked to fit
-- under it at fresh parameters; an undeclared one is the fields' level as before. Before, a data
-- type's level had to be closed and equal its fields', so a list of types or a unit at level l could
-- not be declared. experiments/levels/data-levels.cub: UnitL l : Type l, LstL l A : Type l with a
-- list of types, a field above the declared universe refused.
-- The sort (2026-11-14, each file read for why it exists; since 2026-11-15 each file of lib/ and src/
-- opens with a line naming its layer by this sort, and lib/ is in directories by layer: core/,
-- bootstrap/, capacities/, library/, examples/, languages/, usage/):
-- Core, the primitive language and its implementation: src/ (the machine: net.c, lang.c, syntax.c,
-- dnf.c, big.c; store.c and sha256.c the store; drivers.c the operating-system side of #run),
-- lib/core/prelude.cub (each type former's composition as a term), lib/core/code.cub (Code and eval-g, which the
-- paper's §1.2 “Programs about programs” puts in the bootstrap), lib/core/check.cub (the checker as a term),
-- lib/core/primitives.cub (after the checker: the core's own data types checked, Path, and the primitives
-- declared at checked types: guarded recursion's and reflection's).
-- In the core's load order but not core: lib/bootstrap/match.cub, lib/bootstrap/nav.cub, lib/bootstrap/cost.cub, lib/bootstrap/lin.cub (loaded
-- before the checker as bootstrap files; rewriting, navigation and linear arithmetic are the
-- specializer's and the decision procedures' business. Read 2026-11-14: the checker uses only
-- andb, orb, notb, omap (match.cub) and obind (lin.cub); the four files are in the bootstrap because
-- they are untyped (no signatures), which the checker refuses outside it. They leave the core when
-- written with types, those five helpers moving into lib/core/code.cub); lib/library/std.cub is the library, and lib/core.cub is a load list named
-- core while it loads the library. Misplaced in the core: lib/bootstrap/prim.cub's Word, Int64, UInt32, UInt64,
-- Float64, Float32 and Bytes n with their %-operations in src/lang.c and src/big.c (machine
-- representations, the examples' kind: definitions in the library, with native speed in the evaluator
-- under a relation), foreign (FFI) and ptr-of/region-at (the world, not the language), and #jit binding
-- x86 bytes with no relation (src/lang.c's native path).
-- Capacities demonstrated and mentioned in the paper: lib/capacities/nbe.cub (§6.1 typed quotation and readback),
-- lib/capacities/codes.cub, lib/capacities/specialize.cub, lib/capacities/rules.cub (§6.2 the specializer), lib/capacities/machine.cub, lib/capacities/x86.cub,
-- lib/capacities/elf.cub, lib/capacities/compile/ (native realization by x86, superseded by the route through C).
-- Library, shared mathematics over the core: lib/library/std.cub (its 12 core primitive declarations, the
-- clocks' dfix, pfix and forceκ and the checker's reflection def-code to codata-recs, moved 2026-11-15 to
-- lib/core/primitives.cub, loaded right after the checker, with the #check-data of the core's own data
-- types and Path, pfix's type stated through Path and dfix-next instead of std's _≡_, next and fix; its
-- 5 primitives that are not core, code-map, code-shift, code-unshift, rule-rewrite and rules-orient,
-- lib/bootstrap/match.cub's untyped helpers made trusted primitives, moved to lib/bootstrap/exposed.cub,
-- where they stay until match.cub is written with types; std is no longer admitted to hold primitives), lib/library/fiber.cub, lib/library/sort.cub, lib/library/proc.cub
-- (§7.6 processes and handlers), lib/library/linproof.cub, lib/library/eqproof.cub, lib/library/ring.cub, lib/library/intproof.cub,
-- lib/library/intlin.cub (decision procedures with their certificates), lib/library/derive.cub (deriving, refused
-- until typed).
-- Examples, what the paper shows the language doing: lib/examples/autodiff.cub, lib/examples/decode.cub, lib/examples/region.cub,
-- lib/examples/obs.cub, lib/examples/session.cub, lib/examples/choreo.cub, lib/examples/fleet.cub, lib/examples/net.cub, lib/examples/deploy.cub,
-- experiments/brunerie, experiments/fleet, experiments/obs.
-- Language support (goal 3, lifted languages): lib/languages/bits.cub, lib/languages/isa/, lib/languages/mem/, lib/languages/os/, lib/languages/cxx/,
-- lib/languages/lang/rust/, lib/languages/world.cub (the drivers' signature), experiments/isa, mem, os, cxx, rust, tso,
-- native, machine, jit.
-- Our usage: lib/usage/task-graph.cub, lib/usage/document.cub, task-graph/, lib/library.cub and lib/core.cub (load
-- lists), the Makefile, ./hyperactive and tools/ (Python where it should be the language).
language-implementation-remainder : Node
language-implementation-remainder = node "Everything else the paper specifies for the language: the calculus conforming to its published rules, the specializer with its law, native realization, the laws of the primitives, deriving as a typed term, and terms of any depth." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- paper-examples (2026-11-13, Avik): "term-by-term construction of every single construction
-- including examples, appendix, in paper, as code in repo cited by content-address in paper ...
-- expressely de-prioritized ... while this *serves nothing*." It serves no node but the root, so
-- the order of work gives it nothing while anything else is open. The paper is Avik's current one;
-- the repository's copy, an older draft, is hyperactive-paper (below).
paper-examples : Node
paper-examples = node "Every construction in the paper, its examples and appendix included, as a term in the repository, cited from the paper by content address." (cons the-product nil)
-- paper-matches-implementation (2026-11-14, Avik): "All impl details in paper will be updated to match
-- all impl details in repo when impl is done. Use the work system to ensure this is so. Then don't
-- have to worry about that maximally uninteresting bullshit until everything interesting is done."
-- Every description in the paper of how something is implemented (e.g. its native-code section's
-- x86 encoder, mn-run and Correct1, which the C route replaces) is rewritten to what the repository
-- implements. It waits on the language implementation and the product implementation (after, below).
paper-matches-implementation : Node
paper-matches-implementation = node "Every implementation detail the paper states is updated to match the repository's implementation." (cons the-product nil)
-- product-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "all the product work of building a live interactive
-- collaboration system & tooling over the language & its terms". How it is held, changed and shared:
-- “State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md).
-- The product is not a system built on the language and has no parts: it is the language's own
-- notions taken seriously, one organism (Avik, 2026-11-13). A definition's identity is its content;
-- so a change is a new term, related to the old by a path or a square in the language; so nothing is
-- overwritten and there is no canonical state to merge into; so a view is a term the language
-- evaluates over the growing set of terms; and the interface is those views rendered for a person.
-- Each follows from the one before; none is built apart from the others.
-- What is known about this goal follows (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): facts,
-- not a breakdown and not parts; the session working on the goal decides how to break it down when its
-- work begins (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)).
-- Known (2026-11-13, Avik's correction): identity is semantic, two terms being the same exactly
-- when their content is; SHA-256 equality is one witness of it, not something interpretation
-- computes. Inside a run the evaluator's sound representation is interning (terms built from equal
-- parts share one node, so pointer equality is content identity, proved once about the interning);
-- a hash is needed only where identity crosses what a pointer cannot (another run, another machine,
-- the store), under collision resistance as a named assumption. So the findings below that ask for
-- every reference to be keyed by its content hash name the wrong fix: what is missing is
-- translating an evaluator result's run-local identity into a portable one when it leaves the run.
-- This is the paper's pattern throughout (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…: native code
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, primitives §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, digests §10.8 “Content-addressed definitions, mathematical identity, and a decentralized codebase”): a mechanism (native code, a primitive, a pointer, a hash) is
-- admitted only with its relation to the defined meaning.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the change-management reasoning): holding an object's content address
-- cannot be what grants seeing it, because the address of low-entropy content (a short message, a
-- small edit, a number) is guessable by hashing candidates; so visibility is decided where an
-- object crosses a boundary, the same place run-local identity becomes portable identity, and an
-- object whose audience is restricted gets a portable key that only its audience can compute, which
-- gives up sharing it with identical objects outside that audience and changes nothing about its
-- semantic identity.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work leaves git for the change
-- management Avik designed. The design is “State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md); his words are “To the coordinating session, on state, change and collaboration (2026-11-13, forwarded to this one)” (AI-AGENTS-WELCOME-TO-HYPERACTIVITY.md),
-- whose headings are cited here (they were sections 1 to 8 of docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md,
-- since folded into it): the work is a collection of immutable objects, not a tree of files ("Our
-- state is not text"); a session reads and writes through a query interface and materializes
-- locally only to act, an executable and its input ("A work session: read, write, act"); what a
-- session does internally stays invisible to Avik's views ("Visibility"); there is no canonical
-- state and nothing is promoted, every change being new code ("There is no canonical state", "Code
-- is immutable and content-addressed"); old objects are marked dead by a statement with a reason
-- ("Old objects, and marking them dead"); a change to an inner construction relates its users' old
-- and new versions by a square ("Change is a square"); questions are tasks with reasons ("Questions
-- are tasks"). Git and Pijul are the wrong base ("Code is immutable and content-addressed"). Until it exists, git carries the work: one session, on
-- main only, no branch, every change pushed the moment it is made (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every definition and every result
-- is identified by its content and kept, so work is shared across runs and collaborators.
-- definitions and results identified by content, the foundation of collaboration with no canonical
-- state (“State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). A reference is keyed by its index in the run
-- (src/net.c), so no result is kept or shared beyond a run (src/lang.c); the checker's identity is
-- a hash of file texts (src/store.c); the standard commands never use the store (Makefile). Elaborations
-- are already remembered (a warm library check, 4.6 s). It also makes every check fast, which
-- speeds all other work.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Every evaluator
-- result read back here (checks, the elaborator's solving rounds via %chk-def / %infer-ok, usages,
-- readings) lives only for this run. Each is to be kept beyond it in canonical form: the query
-- identified by its canonical key (references as content keys; the checker identified by its
-- own definitions' keys), the result stored with references as keys and rendered through the
-- current bindings when read, the same mechanism as the store's definitions. The store's
-- certificates are then such results; the per-file cache goes (gone 2026-11-12). Read 2026-11-12:
-- the query built here (build_top) is keyed in src/net.c by synkey, but that key's leaves are run-
-- local: a definition by its global index (key_global), a λ or thunk by the address of its compiled
-- record (K_LAM over t->rec), and the codes passed name checked definitions by their index (cglob
-- i). So the evaluator's key of a query means nothing in another run. Two key schemes exist: the
-- evaluator's (structural, run-local) and the store's (canonical forms, content keys). The end
-- state is one: every key canonical, compiled records keyed by their content. The first step
-- can be taken here: the query's key formed as the store forms a definition's (its canonical form,
-- references as content keys, plus the checker's identity), and the result stored canonically.
-- 2026-11-12: one kind of result is kept so: a declaration's whole elaboration (src/syntax.c,
-- x_checked; below, done). The evaluator's own results, %chk-def's and the readings', are not yet.
-- Read 2026-11-12, for the elaborator's case (src/syntax.c x_checked, the round loop): the right
-- unit is the whole elaboration of a declaration, its input the declaration's surface forms (sig,
-- body) with every name resolved to the key it denotes, its output the solved core forms T and t.
-- The final forms then go through `checked` as now, so the certificate check (key_def,
-- store_checked) still decides acceptance: a remembered elaboration can only change which term is
-- checked, never whether a term is accepted. What is missing is a faithful canonical serialization:
-- canon (src/ store.c) is a key, not a serialization. It drops the type written on a λ's binder
-- (bnd_ty, kept on the name node), instance flags and binder names, and prints a λ's binders so
-- their count cannot be read back. The store needs one canonical form that is both the key and
-- readable back (references as keys, binders nameless but counted, binder types and flags kept),
-- rendered through the current bindings when read; with it canon is that form's hash and nothing is
-- duplicated. Done 2026-11-12 (branch product-implementation, merged): canon's binders readable
-- back and decanon (every library definition round-trips); generated names numbered by first
-- occurrence in an elaboration's key and in its stored form, binders' kept names included (x-case-
-- chain had failed warm, "rec of a name that is not a recursive field of its branch", its pattern's
-- field renumbered while the rebuilt scrutinee's let kept the raw name that `rec` resolves); data
-- keys nominal; the elaboration remembered. Acceptance is not remembered with it: a
-- remembered elaboration is a term like any other, accepted by its own key's certificate or by the
-- checker (the first version marked a hit as already checked, which would have accepted a stale
-- elaboration unchecked). Measured, the library with the store warm and the per-file cache cold:
-- 4.2 s of CPU, no evaluator step, the same 1907 passed and 52 failed as cold (29 s before).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). A reference to a
-- definition is keyed by its index in the current run, so no key involving one means the same thing
-- in another run, and no evaluator result mentioning a definition can be remembered beyond the run.
-- The key of a reference is to be the definition's content key (src/store.c's key of its canonical
-- form); equal content is equal meaning, so this is sound for values. Results that name definitions
-- (readback text, elaboration solutions) are then stored canonically and rendered through current
-- bindings. 2026-11-12: a λ or thunk is likewise keyed by the address of its compiled record
-- (synkey, K_LAM over t->rec), so compiled records need content keys too for any key to be
-- canonical.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/store.c): the checker's identity here is the hash of the texts of the
-- files loaded up to the checker's definition (comments and unrelated definitions included) plus
-- the machine binary, not the content keys of the definitions the checker consists of. So a comment
-- edited in the bootstrap files invalidates every certificate in the store.
-- Known (2026-11-13, at src/store.c): wrong. store_write writes an entry in place (fopen "w"). A
-- machine killed while writing (measured 2026-11-13: the kernel killed one for lack of memory while
-- four read in parallel) leaves a truncated entry that later runs read as a whole one, and two
-- machines on one store can read each other's half-written entries; the store is a cache whose
-- entries decide what is not checked again, so a truncated entry could change a result. Fix: write
-- to a temporary file in the same directory and rename it over the entry (atomic on POSIX). Until
-- then tools/task- graph.py gives each parallel reader its own store. Fixed 2026-11-14: store_write
-- writes the whole entry (an append the old text and the new) to a temporary file beside it and
-- renames it over the entry.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at Makefile): every target sets CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1, so the standard commands
-- never use the store and recheck everything from nothing (64 s for the library). With the store
-- warm, elaboration and compilation still run for every definition (29 s), so incremental checking
-- reaches only the checker's run, not the rest of the work done per definition. Measured again
-- 2026-11-12, the elaboration and the checker's answers (accepted or refused) kept in the store,
-- the per-file cache gone: with the store warm the library takes 4.6 s, its output the fresh run's
-- but for step counts; what remains per run is compilation (compiled records keyed by content,
--). The targets still check from nothing.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work is a value of the
-- language: every declaration's code and reason readable by views, and the interface reads it.
-- serve mode gives views every declaration of a type as a value, but not each declaration's code
-- (src/lang.c); most declarations have no recorded reason (lib/task- graph.cub). The interface for
-- Avik (no editor, no IDE) reads the work through it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Serve mode is how
-- agents and people use the work continuously. Missing: the work given to a term as a value (the
-- declarations and their bindings, each declaration's code), so that views are terms of the
-- language applied to it, and a reading that evaluates a view. Replacing a binding is #retract then
-- a new declaration. 2026-11-12: `declarations T` is every checked definition of type
-- T in scope, as a list (src/ syntax.c), so views over values of a type are terms (lib/task-
-- graph.cub's over Node) and `#show` of one is the reading that evaluates it (tools/task-graph.py
-- view). Still missing: each declaration's code as a value, so that a view can read the code
-- itself, every declaration of every type.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/usage/task-graph.cub): The work as one graph of nodes. Done: goals and
-- tickets are one kind of node (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub); every declaration of the code is a
-- node of the same graph, its reason computed from the machine's reading `#uses` (a declaration
-- with a reason mentions it), from the machine naming it (src/), or stated in the comment above it
-- as `(part of NODE)` or `(prerequisite to NODE)` (tools/task-graph.py why, unexplained). Measured
-- 2026-11-12 over every file: 4310 of 8312 declarations have no reason; stating the rest where the
-- code's text establishes a reason is what remains (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). Library
-- API nothing uses (lib/library/std.cub's pow, fact, happly, the univalence lemmas; lib/core/prelude.cub's
-- untyped helpers) states none: whether each stays is open.
-- Known (2026-11-14, Avik): `hyperactive` is the primary work interface and is built first, since it
-- accelerates all further work; the README holds only setup and hands off to it. Done (2026-11-14):
-- `hyperactive` with no argument begins the walk, a deterministic sequence of steps (Avik's
-- instructions to agents, the paper in full, docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md in full, his words verbatim, the
-- task graph, then what can be taken up), each printed whole and ending with the one command for the
-- next; `hyperactive def NAME` reads a definition by name with its statement and what uses it, with
-- no file or position needed. Not yet: writing through `hyperactive`, code read only by query (the
-- walk and def still print file paths), and onboarding into the core code.
-- Done (2026-11-14, Avik: "an immediate invaluable tool"; source replaced 2026-11-14, Avik: "Absolute
-- total coverage up to modernity"): `hyperactive etym TEXT` returns any text with every word augmented
-- by its etymology, so agents read Avik's words in the meanings the words carry. Source: English
-- Wiktionary (snapshot 2026-10-01, CC BY-SA 4.0) as rendered in the Wikimedia Enterprise HTML dumps,
-- packaged per language by xxyzz/wiktionary_stardict (GitHub release 20261029), fetched once into
-- build/ and indexed into build/etym.sqlite: 450,125 of 817,247 English headwords have an etymology,
-- coinages to the present included (empathy, genocide, apoptosis, neurotypical); each origin is
-- followed through 50 other languages' entries (Latin, Ancient Greek, French, Old English, Arabic …),
-- terms in their own script with transliteration and gloss; 200,829 chains end at a
-- Proto-Indo-European root, 257,930 at some reconstructed root. GCIDE (Webster's 1913) is the
-- fallback for the 9,280 words only it has. Not yet: Wiktionary's reconstruction pages (not in the
-- dumps; a root's sense is the gloss its citing entries give), so chains stop where a reconstruction
-- page would continue them (Proto-Hellenic *génos); a word's later etymology paragraphs (an
-- alternative or deeper root given there) are shown but not followed; kaikki.org (wiktextract) and
-- dumps.wikimedia.org are unreachable from these sessions.
-- Known (2026-11-14, at tools/task-graph.py): needs-work. `hyperactive why` and `hyperactive
-- unexplained` read every file through the machine before answering, and took over ten minutes
-- without finishing on 2026-11-14, so the check that every top-level object of the code has a reason
-- is not usable from the interface.
product-implementation : Node
product-implementation = node "The live interactive collaboration system and tooling over the language and its terms." (cons the-product nil)
-- language-support (2026-11-13, Avik): "Languages support (semantics/syntax as terms for particular
-- languages) * RISC x86, C++, C, Rust, OCaml, Python etc". "before we start any such work, certain
-- things need to be at perfection": it waits on language-implementation and product-implementation
-- (after, below).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at experiments/isa/build-run.cub): needs-work. The imported x86 model has no
-- translation of the two-byte opcode 0F AE (mfence and its group), an inventory gap of the Sail
-- import; any binary using it stops there under the model.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/languages/isa/tso.cub): question. TsoSound is stated and has no inhabitant: no
-- definition proves it. The litmus files in experiments/tso establish instances by computation for
-- particular threads and bounded schedules only. Likewise AtomsX86Correct in lib/languages/mem/rc11.cub.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/languages/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. The C++ reading has drifted from what it
-- stands on, measured 2026-11-12 with each file loaded alone after its imports (--read): here 21
-- failures (type mismatches at lines 64 and 78 and others; a primitive declared outside the
-- bootstrap files, refused); lib/languages/cxx/read-expr.cub 70 (its cases miss constructors lib/languages/cxx/ast.cub
-- gained later: ce-string- literal-units, ck-FloatingCast, clayout-v, ctk-preferred-alignof);
-- experiments/mem/sum.cub (a record field mem-allocs and ev-run-pure, ev-value that no longer
-- exist). The renaming of lib/languages/mem/scalar.cub's operators to ao-... had not reached these files or
-- lib/languages/lang/rust/read- rvalue.cub; done 2026-11-12. The readers are to be brought to the AST and the
-- memory as they are.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/languages/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. Measured 2026-11-11 (memory files, then this
-- file and read-expr.cub, after the library): this file 21 failures, read-expr.cub 70. Roots: cx-
-- unsupported (here) and cx-run (below) do not check against CxEv's type; the primitive cx-byte is
-- refused (primitives exist only in the bootstrap files); the rest follow from these (cx-sk, cx-
-- size undefined; implicit arguments applied to functions without them). The C++ reading does not
-- check as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/languages/cxx/read-expr.cub): wrong. This file refers to read.cub (statements,
-- declarations, the fuel that ties calls) for meanings it does not define; no lib/languages/cxx/read.cub
-- exists. The C++ reading is incomplete as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/languages/lang/rust/machine.cub): needs-work. Measured 2026-11-11: these deriving
-- lines fail because lib/library/derive.cub, which deriving needs, is refused as untyped; the Rust
-- readers then fail on what follows (machine 6, read-place 21, read-rvalue 26, read 22 failures).
-- Typing derive.cub repairs the root.
language-support : Node
language-support = node "The syntax and semantics of particular languages as terms: x86, C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and others." (cons the-product nil)
